 1908: 
 1909: // /**
 1910: //    For a point (x1,y1) inside the element and a line segment (x1,y1)-(x2,y2), find the intersection point 
 1911: //    (xIntersect,yIntersect) and the side (intersectingSide) that it intersects.
 1912: 
 1913: //    Inputs:
 1914: //    @param x1, y1: point inside the element
 1915: //    @param x2, y2: other end of the line segment
 1916: 
 1917: //    Outputs:
 1918: //    @param intersectingSide:  side that intersects the line segment
 1919: //    @param nextElement: element that contains the intersection point
 1920: //    @param xIntersect, yIntersect: intersection point  
 1921: 
 1922: //    The equations for the two segments (the one we are considering and one of the sides of the element) are:
 1923: //    P1(tt1) = (x1,y1) + tt1*(x2-x1, y2-y1)  where 0 <= tt1 <= 1
 1924: //    P2(tt2) = (x3,y3) + tt2*(x4-x3, y4-y3)  where 0 <= tt2 <= 1
 1925: 
 1926: //    If the two segments are parallel, they do not intersect. This is checked by the determinant of the two vectors
 1927: //    (Delta) being zero.
 1928: 
 1929: //    The intersection point happens when:
 1930: //    tt1(x2-x1) - tt2(x4-x3) = x3-x1
 1931: //    tt1(y2-y1) - tt2(y4-y3) = y3-y1
 1932:    
 1933: //    Assumption: the 4 points (including those of the side segment) are different.
 1934: //    The eqs. for the 2 lines are: X=X1+(X2-X1)*tt1 and X=X3+(X4-X3)*tt2.	
 1935: //    Output: false: no intersection or colinear; true: exactly 1 intersection.	
 1936: //    If true, (xIntersect,yIntersect) is the intersection.				
 1937: //    Modified to only check tt2, assuming 0<=tt1<=1 is already assured. 
 1938: // **/
 1939: // __inline__ __device__ bool Element::findPathIntersection(double x1, double y1, double x2, double y2, 
 1940: //                                                          Side& intersectingSide, Element& nextElement,
 1941: //                                                          double& xIntersect, double& yIntersect) {
